In obtuse $\Delta ABC$, altitude $CD = h$ meets $BA$ extended at $D$. Using right-angled $\Delta CAD$ and the supplementary angle identity $\sin(180^{\circ} - A) = \sin A$, prove that $h = b\sin A$ and hence that the sine rule $\dfrac{a}{\sin A} = \dfrac{b}{\sin B}$ holds for obtuse triangles.

