[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (D) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (1.8,0);
\coordinate (B) at (5.5,0);
\coordinate (C) at (0,2.8);
\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway, below] {$c$};
\draw[dashed] (D) -- (A) node[midway, below] {$d$};
\draw[dashed] (C) -- (D) node[midway, left] {$h$};
\draw (A) -- (C) node[midway, above right] {$b$};
\draw (B) -- (C) node[midway, above right] {$a$};
\draw (0,0.3) -- (0.3,0.3) -- (0.3,0);
\pic[draw, "$A$", angle radius=0.45cm, angle eccentricity=1.6] {angle=B--A--C};
\node[below] at (D) {$D$};
\node[below] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above] at (C) {$C$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
In obtuse $\Delta ABC$, altitude $CD = h$ meets $BA$ extended at $D$, so $BD = c + d$. In right $\Delta CAD$, $d = b\cos(180^{\circ} - A) = -b\cos A$ and $h = b\sin A$. Use Pythagoras in $\Delta CBD$ to prove $a^{2} = b^{2} + c^{2} - 2bc\cos A$.
